# NOTES
# Structural findings from this batch:
# 1. Confirmed bug class: the heuristic's plant-level single-vendor assumption breaks specifically at the boundary between the original vendor consortium and later single-vendor branding. Five units (Mihama 1, Ohi 1-2, Takahama 1) were originally built by WESTINGHOUSE, not MHI - the DB mislabeled all Mihama/Ohi/Takahama units as MHI regardless of unit-level vintage. This is a distinct failure mode from the BWR unit-splitting the task anticipated: it's an *early-vs-late* vintage split within the "MHI plant" family, not a same-era multi-vendor split.
# 2. Kashiwazaki-Kariwa Unit 7 and (likely) other ABWR units are joint Hitachi/Toshiba/GE NSSS designs, not single-vendor - the DB's single-Hitachi label is an oversimplification for ABWR specifically (cf. Shimane 3's "Hitachi-GE" label, which correctly captures a joint-venture name but still omits Toshiba/GE where applicable).
# 3. Tokai 1 and Tokai 2 (Japan's two oldest reactors) were both multi-party consortium builds (GEC-UK/GE + Fuji Electric/Hitachi + Shimizu Corporation as general contractor) - the DB collapsed each to a single foreign NSSS vendor name and omitted the Japanese construction partners entirely for both units.
# 4. RPV manufacturer (JSW vs IHI vs foreign) was UNVERIFIABLE for every single unit in this batch (30/30 non-Monju units) - neither English nor Japanese Wikipedia plant articles break out RPV fabricator at all. IAEA PRIS (the authoritative source with explicit per-unit "Turbine Supplier"/"NSSS Supplier" fields) could not be reached this session (403 errors / JS-postback navigation WebFetch cannot follow) and WebSearch quota was exhausted mid-batch. Turbine supplier was similarly UNVERIFIABLE for all 31 units. Recommend a follow-up pass targeting IAEA PRIS directly (via browser) or JAIF/utility primary sources specifically for these two fields.
# 5. Monju (fast breeder reactor) was built by a 4-company consortium (Hitachi/Toshiba/MHI/Fuji Electric) under Fast Reactor Engineering Co., not MHI alone - the DB's non-standard RPV=MHI entry (vs JSW default) could not be confirmed or refuted given the consortium structure.
# 6. Onagawa's Toshiba-only constructor claim and Sendai/Tomari/Shimane's single-vendor claims for constructor DID hold up under Wikipedia verification - the heuristic is not wrong everywhere, just at the vintage-split and joint-NSSS boundaries noted above.
